window.CS_NARR=window.CS_NARR||{};window.CS_NARR["ovta"]={"ovtaatlas": [[null, ["Hi, Buddy here. This sheet condenses OpenAlgo's Open Varsity course on technical analysis. What makes this course unusual is that it tests the popular signals on 16 years of daily data for Nifty 50 stocks, so you'll hear real numbers about what works and what barely does. As always, this is education, not investment advice. Let's look at the map first."]], ["open-varsity-technical-analysis-topic-atlas", ["Here's why the sheet runs in this order. Charts come first, because every pattern and indicator is just a summary of price and volume. Structure and levels, trends, support and resistance, are the foundation for candles and chart patterns. Indicators come after, as confirmation rather than the main event. And analysis to action closes the sheet: timeframes, systems, risk and discipline, which turn a chart read into a trade."]], ["how-to-use-this-sheet", ["Start with charts and structure, because every pattern and indicator is just a summary of price and volume. Then measure every signal against a baseline. The course's study puts the average Nifty 50 stock's drift at about plus 1.48% over every 20 trading days. That's what you'd get by holding on any random day, so a signal that can't beat it has no edge. Before a trade, run the context checklist and size the position from the stop. And if you want to go deeper, your own Technical Analysis sheet covers more patterns, Wyckoff and breadth."]], ["the-course-s-honest-scoreboard", ["Here's the honest scoreboard, from Nifty 50 stocks between 2010 and 2026. The baseline, any day, is plus 1.48% over 20 days. After a golden cross, the stock was higher 60 days later 59% of the time. After a death cross, it was higher 66% of the time, more often than after the bullish signal. RSI above 80, supposedly overbought, gave the best 20 day returns of any RSI bucket, about plus 2.2%. Of 20 day breakouts, 56% fell back inside within 5 days, and even breakouts on more than one and a half times average volume failed 47% of the time. A bullish MACD cross was positive after 30 days 58% of the time. The takeaway: most popular signals barely beat simply holding. The real edge in technical analysis is risk control and consistency, not prediction.", "The takeaway: Against a baseline drift of about +1.5% per 20 days, most popular signals add little; golden crosses and breakouts underwhelm.", "Pause and answer: What's the baseline drift for an average Nifty 50 stock over 20 days?", "⏸", "Answer: About +1.48%."]]], "charts": [[null, ["Section one: getting started. What technical analysis is, chart types, candles and timeframes."]], ["what-technical-analysis-is-and-isn-t", ["Technical analysis is the study of price and volume to tilt the odds and limit losses. It's probability, not prophecy. It rests on three working assumptions, which are tendencies rather than laws: price reflects known information; trends persist until something real changes; and crowd behaviour repeats, so patterns recur, though never identically. Charts can show you the trend and its health, mark levels where the crowd reacted before, and show where your idea is wrong. They can't predict tomorrow, guarantee a pattern, or rescue a position with no exit plan. The real edge comes from predefined exits, repeatable rules instead of moods, and avoiding falling knives.", "The takeaway: TA studies price and volume to tilt the odds and limit losses: probability, not prophecy.", "Pause and answer: Name TA's three working assumptions.", "⏸", "Answer: Price reflects known information, trends persist until something real changes, and crowd behaviour repeats."]], ["technical-vs-fundamental", ["Technical versus fundamental. Fundamental analysis asks what a business is worth, studies the company, and works over months to years, deciding what to own. Technical analysis asks what price is doing and whether it will continue, studies price and volume, and works over days to weeks, deciding when to act. A common pairing is to let fundamentals build the shortlist and technicals time the entry and exit. And always ask \"compared to what?\" Test popular claims instead of believing them.", "The takeaway: Fundamentals decide what to own; technicals decide when to act.", "Pause and answer: Which question does fundamental analysis answer?", "⏸", "Answer: What is it worth?"]], ["chart-types", ["Three chart types. A line chart shows only closes: it gives a clear direction but hides the path within each bar. A bar chart, or OHLC, shows open, high, low and close: complete and compact, but slower to scan. A candlestick shows OHLC plus colour and body, so you see the balance between buyers and sellers at a glance, though it gets busy on dense charts. Before forming any view, check three things: which stock it is, what one bar represents, and the price range from top to bottom. A squeezed axis can make a 1% move look violent.", "The takeaway: Line shows closes, bars show OHLC, candles add colour and body; check stock, bar period and price range first.", "Pause and answer: What does a line chart hide?", "⏸", "Answer: The path within each bar: opens, highs and lows."]], ["reading-a-single-candle", ["Reading a single candle. Colour: a close above the open is green, below is red, showing who won that bar. It compares the open and close of the same bar, so a gap down day can still be green. Body length shows how decisive the win was. Wicks show ground that was traded but not held: a long lower wick means buyers defended lower prices; a long upper wick means sellers defended higher ones. Judge size against recent bars, never in absolute terms. And context, meaning trend, level and volume, decides whether the candle means anything. A marubozu, all body, is one sided conviction; a doji, with almost no body, is balance. But one candle is a whisper, not a signal.", "The takeaway: Colour compares close to open of the same bar, body length shows decisiveness, wicks show ground traded but not held.", "Pause and answer: Can a gap-down day be a green candle?", "⏸", "Answer: Yes, if it closes above its own open."]], ["timeframes-direction-vs-timing", ["Timeframes. For intraday trading you trade on minute charts and check direction on the daily. For swing trades lasting days, you trade on the daily and check direction on the weekly. For long term investing, trade on the weekly or monthly and check the monthly. Decide the holding period first, then the timeframe, never the reverse. The higher timeframe sets direction; the lower sets timing. A dip on the hourly inside a daily uptrend is usually just a pullback. The course uses daily charts: one decision a day, enough signals to measure, and less noise from order matching.", "The takeaway: Pick the holding period first; the higher timeframe sets direction, the lower sets timing.", "Pause and answer: A swing trader trades the daily chart. Which chart sets direction?", "⏸", "Answer: The weekly."]], ["scales-and-chart-settings-that-mislead", ["Chart settings can mislead you. On a linear scale, equal rupee moves take equal height. On a log scale, equal percentage moves take equal height, so 100 to 200 and 1,000 to 2,000 look the same, both plus 100%. Use log for long, large percentage moves and linear for short, tight ranges. A truncated axis and auto scaling can exaggerate or shrink identical candles, so read the axis numbers first. The date window matters too: start at a crash low and anything looks heroic; start at a peak and it looks broken. And aggregation, where a week or month is cut, can even flip a weekly candle's colour.", "The takeaway: Log scale shows equal percentage moves equally; truncated axes and date windows can exaggerate.", "Pause and answer: On a log scale, how do 100→200 and 1,000→2,000 compare?", "⏸", "Answer: They look the same height: both +100%."]]], "structure": [[null, ["Section two: structure and levels. Dow theory, trends, support and resistance, breakouts, and Fibonacci."]], ["dow-theory-six-tenets-and-three-trends", ["Dow theory has six tenets. Prices already reflect known information. Three trends run at once. Primary trends move through phases. The averages must confirm each other. Volume must confirm the trend. And a trend holds until there's a clear reversal. The three trends: the primary trend lasts months to years and builds or destroys wealth. The secondary trend lasts weeks and retraces about a third to two thirds of the prior move. And the minor trend lasts days; it's noise, useful only for timing.", "The takeaway: Prices discount news, three trends run at once, averages and volume must confirm, and a trend holds until a clear reversal.", "Pause and answer: Name Dow's three trends.", "⏸", "Answer: Primary (months to years), secondary (weeks), minor (days)."]], ["market-cycle-and-the-volume-rule", ["The market cycle goes from accumulation, a quiet base, to markup, with higher highs and lows, to distribution, a churning top, to markdown, with lower highs and lows, and back to accumulation. The volume rule: volume should grow with the main trend and fade on counter moves. Thin rallies or heavy declines are warnings, not reversal triggers. And be humble: these phases are clearest in hindsight, so don't try to label them in real time.", "The takeaway: Accumulation, markup, distribution, markdown; volume should grow with the trend and fade on counter-moves.", "Pause and answer: Which phase follows distribution?", "⏸", "Answer: Markdown."]], ["a-dow-trend-filter-vs-buy-and-hold", ["The course tested a Dow style trend filter on 48 Nifty 50 stocks over 16 years. The rule: hold only while the price is above the 200 day average and the 50 day is above the 200 day. The filter's median return was about 107%, against about 744% for buy and hold. Its median maximum drawdown was about 47% versus 56%. It beat buy and hold on only 4 of 48 stocks, and it spent about 45% of the time out of the market. So Dow style filters are a risk management framework, not a profit machine. They trimmed drawdowns, but gave up most of the return.", "The takeaway: A trend filter reduced drawdown a little but sacrificed most of the return; it's risk management, not a profit machine.", "Pause and answer: How did the 50/200 filter compare with buy-and-hold on returns?", "⏸", "Answer: About +107% median versus about +744%."]], ["trends-and-trendlines", ["Trends and trendlines. Name the state first. In an uptrend, with higher highs and lows, buy dips. In a downtrend, with lower highs and lows, don't buy weakness. Sideways, buy the floor and sell the ceiling. Trendlines join rising swing lows in an uptrend, or falling swing highs in a downtrend. Two points draw a line and a third confirms it; three or four clean touches is ideal, while eight means it's tired. Never force it or keep redrawing it. A gentle slope can last months, while a near vertical angle is exhaustion risk, not strength; use a log scale on long charts. Note the difference: a trendline break means the angle changed, but a broken swing low means the trend changed, so decide on swing points, not lines. And one test: a dip into a rising 50 day average returned about 0.67% over the next 10 days, against 0.58% for any uptrend day. Real, but tiny.", "The takeaway: Name the trend state first; trendlines join swing lows or highs, and two points draw, a third confirms.", "Pause and answer: In a downtrend, should you buy weakness?", "⏸", "Answer: No: lower highs and lows mean don't buy dips."]], ["pullback-or-trend-change", ["This ladder separates a pullback from a trend change. Price is dipping inside an uptrend. First: is the prior higher low still intact? If not, treat it as a trend change, broken until proven otherwise. If it is intact: is volume light on the dip? If volume is heavy, be cautious and wait for it to stabilise. If it's light: is the larger timeframe trend still up? If not, it's a counter trend bounce risk, so use smaller size or stand aside. If yes, it's likely a pullback: hold, or plan an entry with a stop below the higher low.", "The takeaway: Higher low intact, light volume on the dip and an intact higher-timeframe trend say pullback.", "Pause and answer: The prior higher low breaks. What then?", "⏸", "Answer: Treat it as a trend change until proven otherwise."]], ["support-and-resistance", ["Support and resistance. Why do levels work? Traders who profited there buy more, traders who missed it buy on the return, and trapped holders sell at breakeven. Levels come from prior swing highs and lows, which matter most, round numbers like 500 or 1,000, and long sideways bases. Draw zones, not lines: mark the cluster of highs or lows, not the single extreme tick. A practical start is the highest high and lowest low of the last 20 sessions. Role reversal: broken resistance tends to become support and vice versa, and the retest is usually the cleaner entry. But many tests weaken a level, because each test absorbs orders. A level hit 13 or 14 times is a countdown to a break, not a stronger wall. And confluence, two independent reasons at one price, makes a zone stronger.", "The takeaway: Levels work because of trader memory; draw zones from prior swings, round numbers and bases, not single lines.", "Pause and answer: Why does broken resistance often become support?", "⏸", "Answer: Traders who missed it buy the return, and those who profited add more."]], ["ranges-channels-and-breakouts", ["Ranges, channels and breakouts. A range is a sideways box; a channel is the same idea with parallel sloping lines. Markets spend roughly two thirds of their time sideways. Inside a range, buy near support, sell near resistance, avoid the middle, and put the stop just outside the box. Interestingly, a close above the upper Bollinger band still averaged about plus 0.58% over 10 days, so touching an edge isn't automatically a reversal. On breakouts to a new 20 day high, 56% fell back inside within 5 sessions, and even on volume above one and a half times average, 47% failed. So treat fresh breakouts as guilty until proven innocent: wait for a close beyond the level, then the retest.", "The takeaway: Markets spend about two-thirds of their time sideways; buy near support, sell near resistance, avoid the middle.", "Pause and answer: Where does the stop go on a range trade?", "⏸", "Answer: Just outside the box."]], ["trading-the-retest", ["Here's how to trade the retest. Price closes beyond the range. You wait for it to return to the level. Then ask: does the old resistance hold as support? If it holds, enter, with a stop on the far side of the level. If price fails back inside the range, it was a fakeout, so stand aside. The retest filters out many failed breakouts, and it gives a tighter stop than chasing the first breakout candle.", "The takeaway: Wait for a breakout to return to the level; enter if old resistance holds as support, stand aside if it fails.", "Pause and answer: Where's the stop on a retest entry?", "⏸", "Answer: On the far side of the level."]], ["fibonacci-the-pullback-map", ["Fibonacci levels are a map of pullbacks. 23.6% is a shallow dip in a strong trend. 38.2% is the first level most traders watch. 50% isn't actually Fibonacci; it's Dow's halfway level. 61.8% is the deepest normal pullback in a healthy trend. 78.6% is last ditch; beyond it the move is usually broken. And 127.2, 161.8 and 261.8% are extension zones for taking profit. The formula in an uptrend: the level equals the swing high minus the ratio times the swing range. An extension target is the pullback low plus the ratio times the original move.", "The takeaway: 38.2%, 50% and 61.8% are the main pullback levels; beyond 78.6% the move is usually broken.", "Pause and answer: Is 50% a Fibonacci ratio?", "⏸", "Answer: No. It's Dow's halfway level."]], ["fibonacci-worked-example-and-rules", ["A worked example. A swing ran from 553.5 to 1,094.2, a move of about 540. The 38.2% level sits at 887.7, the 50% at 823.9, and the 61.8% at 760. The pullback bottomed near 788, about 57%, inside the 50 to 61.8% decision zone, and then the trend resumed. The rules: a pullback into the zone with a turn signal, like a hammer, an engulfing candle or rising volume, suggests a healthy trend. Slicing through 61.8% and still falling suggests the trend may be over. Pick the swing points first and let the levels fall where they fall; drawing them after deciding to buy is self deception. And confluence strengthens a level: prior support, a rising 50 day average, a round number, or the same level on the weekly chart.", "The takeaway: The 50–61.8% zone is the decision zone; a turn signal there means a healthy trend, slicing through 61.8% means it may be over.", "Pause and answer: Swing 553.5 to 1,094.2: where's the 61.8% level?", "⏸", "Answer: About 760."]]], "candles": [[null, ["Section three: candlestick patterns, and why context decides everything."]], ["single-candles", ["Single candles. A doji has open and close almost equal, with a body under about a tenth of the range; after a long run it signals a momentum pause, not a reversal call. Gravestone and dragonfly dojis have a long upper or long lower wick; at resistance or support they show buying or selling rejected. A spinning top has a small body with wicks both sides: after a strong trend, neither side is winning and fuel is running low. A hammer has a small body on top and a lower wick at least twice the body; after a decline at support, it's a bullish hint. The hanging man is the same shape after a rally at resistance, a bearish warning. A shooting star has a small body at the bottom and a long upper wick; after a rally it's a bearish hint, while the same shape after a decline, an inverted hammer, is tentatively bullish. And a marubozu, all body, shows conviction in breakouts and trend legs. The key lesson: the same shape means opposite things in different places. Shape alone never sets direction.", "The takeaway: Doji, spinning tops, hammers and shooting stars signal pauses or rejection, and matter only at the right place.", "Pause and answer: What does a doji after a long run signal?", "⏸", "Answer: A momentum pause, not a reversal call."]], ["two-and-three-candle-patterns", ["Two and three candle patterns. A harami is a small body inside a big one: momentum stalls, a weak signal to watch. An engulfing pattern, where the second body covers the first, shows control changing hands: moderate weight. A tweezer top or bottom rejects the same high or low twice: moderate at a level. Morning and evening stars use three candles, a takeover plus confirmation, so they carry more weight. And three soldiers or three crows show strong momentum; they confirm, but often late. More candles demand more agreement, so they carry more weight, but reliability stays modest. One winning example, a bullish engulfing at a swing low followed by a 5.7% rise in 10 sessions, is an illustration, not a base rate.", "The takeaway: Harami is weak, engulfing moderate, stars stronger; three soldiers or crows confirm momentum.", "Pause and answer: What does an engulfing pattern show?", "⏸", "Answer: A change of control: the second body covers the first."]], ["the-three-context-filters", ["Three context filters decide whether a pattern matters. Level: strong if it's at support, resistance, a prior high or low, a round number or a Fibonacci level; weak in open space. Trend: strong if it's with the larger trend, at the end of a pullback; weak against an extended trend. And volume: strong when above average, weak on a thin, sleepy session. A hammer in the middle of nowhere means nothing. The same hammer at support, in an uptrend, on heavy volume, is a setup. And counter trend reversal patterns fail far more often.", "The takeaway: Level, trend and volume turn a candle into a setup; the same hammer in open space means nothing.", "Pause and answer: Name the three context filters.", "⏸", "Answer: Level, trend and volume."]], ["should-i-act-on-this-candle-pattern", ["This ladder turns those filters into a decision. A pattern matches your written definition. Is it at a level you marked beforehand? If not, skip it: a pattern in open space is noise. Does it agree with the larger trend? If not, skip it, because counter trend reversals fail far more often. Is volume above average? If not, downgrade it and wait for the next bar to confirm. And is there a clean stop beyond the pattern, with enough reward to justify it? If not, skip: no clean stop, no trade. If it passes all four, it's a setup: define the stop and target before entering, and journal it.", "The takeaway: Act only at a level marked beforehand, with the trend, on above-average volume, with a clean stop.", "Pause and answer: A pattern in open space with no prior level: act?", "⏸", "Answer: Skip it: noise."]], ["pattern-discipline", ["Pattern discipline, in five habits. Mark support, resistance and trend on a clean chart before you look for patterns. Use a fixed, written definition, for example a hammer's lower wick must be at least twice the body. Count independent reasons: one is weak, three make a setup. Define stop and target before entering. And log every pattern and its outcome. Most patterns barely beat the plus 1.48% per 20 days baseline, and a journal exposes your bias.", "The takeaway: Mark levels first, use written definitions, count independent reasons, define stop and target, and log every pattern.", "Pause and answer: How many independent reasons make a setup?", "⏸", "Answer: Three; one is weak."]]], "chartpat": [[null, ["Section four: chart patterns. Reversals, continuations and gaps."]], ["reversal-patterns", ["Reversal patterns. A head and shoulders top has the middle peak highest and the right shoulder below the head; it confirms on a close below the neckline. The inverse head and shoulders has the middle trough lowest and confirms on a close above the neckline. A double top, the M shape, has two similar highs and confirms below the middle dip. A double bottom, the W, has two similar lows and confirms above the middle peak. Triple tops and bottoms test one level three times and confirm on a break; they're rarer and slower. Every reversal needs a clear prior trend to reverse, a defined neckline, and follow through after the break. Volume is typically heaviest on the left shoulder and head, lighter on the right, and expands on the break.", "The takeaway: Head and shoulders and double or triple tops and bottoms confirm only on a close beyond the neckline.", "Pause and answer: When does a head and shoulders top confirm?", "⏸", "Answer: On a close below the neckline."]], ["confirming-a-reversal-with-a-worked-target", ["Confirming a reversal, step by step. Confirm there's a prior trend. Draw the neckline before the last leg completes. Wait for a decisive close beyond it, not an intraday poke. Look for volume expansion, and consider entering on the retest. Put the stop just back inside the pattern. The measured target is the neckline plus or minus the pattern height. Example: a double bottom with lows around 1,080 and a neckline around 1,270 has a height of about 190, so the target is about 1,270 plus 190, roughly 1,460. That target is a planning figure for weighing reward against risk, not a promise.", "The takeaway: Prior trend, neckline drawn in advance, a decisive close, volume, then target = neckline ± pattern height.", "Pause and answer: Double bottom lows about 1,080, neckline about 1,270. Target?", "⏸", "Answer: About 1,460 (1,270 + 190)."]], ["continuation-patterns", ["Continuation patterns. A bull or bear flag is a small drifting rectangle after a sharp move; it leans with the flagpole, and the measured move is the flagpole's height from the breakout. A pennant is a small triangle contracting to a point, leaning with the trend. An ascending triangle has a flat top and rising lows and often breaks up. A descending triangle has a flat bottom and falling highs and often breaks down, though it can break up in an uptrend. A symmetrical triangle, with lower highs and higher lows, is neutral, and the trend guides it. Triangles project their widest height. A rectangle is a flat range inside a trend, ambiguous while forming, projecting its box height. Flags and pennants last days to a few weeks. A healthy pause is shallow and orderly, with volume drying up inside and expanding on the break. A deep, accelerating pullback, or volume swelling against the trend, says reversal instead. And if the target is barely larger than the stop distance, skip the trade.", "The takeaway: Flags, pennants and triangles usually continue the trend; measured moves use the flagpole or widest height.", "Pause and answer: How is a flag's target measured?", "⏸", "Answer: Flagpole height from the breakout."]], ["gaps-four-types", ["Four types of gap. A common gap appears inside a range; it's small and fills quickly. A breakaway gap leaves a base or pattern; it starts a trend and often doesn't fill. A runaway gap appears mid trend, showing acceleration, often near the halfway point. And an exhaustion gap comes at the end of an extended move: a final leap that usually fills and then reverses. Breakaway and exhaustion gaps look identical on the day; only the trend's age and the next few sessions tell them apart. An island reversal, where bars are stranded between two gaps, signals a likely flip.", "The takeaway: Common gaps fill fast, breakaway gaps start trends, runaway gaps accelerate them, exhaustion gaps end them.", "Pause and answer: Which gap type usually fills, then reverses?", "⏸", "Answer: Exhaustion."]], ["gap-statistics-and-rules", ["Gap statistics and rules. On Nifty 50 daily data, gaps over 2% filled within 20 sessions about 78 to 79% of the time, and gaps over 5% about 71 to 73%. Bigger gaps fill less often, so fading them is a bet against the odds. Heavy volume suggests conviction, a breakaway or runaway gap; light volume suggests a common gap. Read every gap with four questions: what news, how big in percent, where in the trend, and how much volume. Don't chase the open; let the first range form, and treat the previous close and the gap open as levels. Size down on gap days, because wider stops need smaller positions to keep rupee risk constant. The gap percentage is the open minus the previous close, divided by the previous close, times 100: from 1,200 to 1,290 is 90 points, or 7.5%.", "The takeaway: Nifty 50 gaps over 2% filled within 20 sessions about 78% of the time; bigger gaps fill less often.", "Pause and answer: Gaps over 5%: roughly how often filled within 20 sessions?", "⏸", "Answer: About 71–73%."]]], "ind": [[null, ["Section five: indicators. Averages, momentum, volatility, volume, and trend strength. This is the longest section, so take it slowly."]], ["moving-averages", ["Moving averages. A simple moving average is the sum of the last N closes divided by N; each new bar adds the newest close and drops the oldest. On daily charts, the 20 day covers about a month and shows short term swing direction; the 50 day covers about a quarter and shows the intermediate trend; and the 200 day covers about a year and shows long term health. The SMA weighs every bar equally, so it's calmer with more lag. The EMA weighs recent bars more, so it turns sooner but twitches falsely more often. You can choose the lag trade off, but never remove it. Read the slope first, rising, flat or falling, then price versus the line; slope beats the exact touch. And a 50 period average on a daily chart isn't the same as on a weekly chart, so always state the timeframe.", "The takeaway: The 20, 50 and 200-day averages track month, quarter and year; SMAs are calm and laggy, EMAs turn faster.", "Pause and answer: Which average represents about a year?", "⏸", "Answer: The 200-day."]], ["golden-and-death-crosses-tested", ["Golden and death crosses, tested over 16 years on the Nifty 50. Of 527 golden crosses, 59% were up after 60 days, with a typical move of plus 2.7%. Of 529 death crosses, 66% were up after 60 days, with a typical move of plus 4.8%. The baseline drift over 60 days was about plus 4.5%. So on this data, \"golden means buy, death means sell\" is close to backwards, and golden cross winners often dipped about 7% first. A dip to a rising 50 day average gave plus 0.67% over 10 days against a 0.58% baseline, an edge under a tenth of a point. The better use of averages is as a direction filter, for example, longs only while the 50 day is above a rising 200 day, which mainly cuts drawdowns and time at risk.", "The takeaway: On 16 years of Nifty 50 data, death crosses were followed by better 60-day returns than golden crosses.", "Pause and answer: After a death cross, how often was the stock higher 60 days later?", "⏸", "Answer: About 66% of the time."]], ["rsi-momentum-not-a-buy-sell-switch", ["RSI is momentum, not a buy and sell switch. RSI, with a default of 14 periods, compares the strength of recent up moves with down moves on a 0 to 100 scale. 50 is balance, and 70 and 30 are the usual reference lines. It compares a stock only with its own history. Shorter lookbacks like 7 are faster and noisier; longer ones like 21 are smoother. In a range, RSI swings between extremes. In an uptrend it tends to hold about 60 to 80, and a break below 40 is a warning. In a downtrend it lives around 20 to 40. The test results: any day gave plus 1.48% over 20 days. Below 30 gave plus 1.8%. 50 to 70 and 70 to 80 gave plus 1.4%. And above 80 gave plus 2.2%, positive 60% of the time. Overbought streaks lasted up to 43 days and oversold streaks up to 44. So extremes are states, often of persistent strength, not reversal signals.", "The takeaway: RSI measures momentum against a stock's own history; in uptrends it holds about 60–80, and 'overbought' can keep rising.", "Pause and answer: What RSI range does an uptrend tend to hold?", "⏸", "Answer: About 60–80."]], ["divergence-and-the-oscillator-family", ["Divergence and the oscillator family. Bearish divergence is when price makes a higher high but RSI makes a lower high. Bullish divergence is price making a lower low while RSI makes a higher low. Divergence can last weeks, so use it at extremes near levels, and let a price trigger, like a broken trendline or reversal candle, time the entry. The other oscillators: the stochastic shows where the close sits in the recent range, with above 80 overbought and below 20 oversold. Williams percent R is an inverted stochastic, from 0 to minus 100. CCI measures distance from the recent average, unbounded, with plus or minus 100 marking stretched. And stochastic RSI is a stochastic of RSI, the most sensitive and noisiest. Remember: five oscillators on the same prices are one opinion repeated, not five confirmations.", "The takeaway: Divergence warns of fading momentum and can last weeks; let a price trigger time the entry.", "Pause and answer: Define bearish divergence.", "⏸", "Answer: Price makes a higher high while RSI makes a lower high."]], ["macd", ["MACD. The MACD line is the 12 period EMA minus the 26 period EMA. The signal line is a 9 period EMA of the MACD line. And the histogram is the MACD line minus the signal line. How to read it: MACD crossing above or below the signal means momentum is turning up or down. MACD above or below zero means the trend leans up or down. A growing histogram means momentum is accelerating; a shrinking histogram means momentum is fading, an early warning before a cross. And divergence means the trend is tiring, so tighten risk and wait for price. Since MACD is measured in rupees, compare shapes and crossings, not raw values across stocks.", "The takeaway: MACD = EMA12 − EMA26, signal = its 9-period EMA; a shrinking histogram warns before a cross.", "Pause and answer: What does a shrinking histogram signal?", "⏸", "Answer: Momentum fading: an early warning before a cross."]], ["macd-crossovers-tested", ["MACD crossovers, tested. 7,640 bullish crosses on Nifty 50 stocks over 16 years: 58% were positive after 30 days, and 37% gained more than 5%. Filtering for MACD above zero did not meaningfully raise the win rate. MACD is strong in trends, where the histogram flags fatigue early, weak in sideways markets with repeated whipsaw crosses, and it always lags. Practical use: confirm the trend with price and averages first, use a bullish cross near support to time a continuation entry, and watch the histogram for fading momentum.", "The takeaway: Bullish MACD crosses were positive 30 days later only about 58% of the time; strong in trends, whipsaws in ranges.", "Pause and answer: Did filtering for MACD above zero raise the win rate?", "⏸", "Answer: Not meaningfully."]], ["volume-relative-volume-and-spikes", ["Volume. Relative volume is today's volume divided by the 20 day average. About 1 is ordinary, 2 is double, and 5 or 6 or more means a major event. A spike with a decisive close shows conviction in the close's direction. A spike at the end of a long trend may be a climax. A spike with a flat close is churn, a draw, not a signal. In a healthy uptrend, volume swells on up days and fades on pullbacks, so rallies on shrinking volume or selloffs on rising volume are warnings. Remember, volume confirms moves; it doesn't start them.", "The takeaway: Relative volume = today ÷ 20-day average; spikes with decisive closes show conviction, flat closes show churn.", "Pause and answer: Relative volume of 5 suggests what?", "⏸", "Answer: A major event."]], ["obv-mfi-a-d-and-cmf", ["Four volume indicators. OBV, on balance volume, adds volume on up days and subtracts it on down days; ignore its level, and check its slope and whether it agrees with price. MFI, the money flow index, is a volume weighted RSI from 0 to 100; above 80 or below 20 is stretched, and divergence is its best signal. The accumulation distribution line weights volume by where the close sits in the day's range, so a close near the high adds the most. And CMF, Chaikin money flow, is an oscillator around zero over about 20 days; above zero, buying pressure dominates. They all measure the same idea, so pick one you trust. In India, also look at delivery percentage. Two stocks both up 3% on four times normal volume mean different things at 70% delivery, where holders are accumulating, and at 15% delivery, which is intraday churn.", "The takeaway: Volume indicators show who is behind a move; for OBV, watch slope and agreement, not the level.", "Pause and answer: What's MFI?", "⏸", "Answer: A volume-weighted RSI on a 0–100 scale."]], ["bollinger-bands-and-atr", ["Bollinger Bands and ATR. The middle band is the 20 day SMA, and the bands sit 2 standard deviations either side. Bandwidth is the upper minus lower band, divided by the middle; small bandwidth means a squeeze. True range is the largest of high minus low, high minus previous close, and low minus previous close, in absolute terms, and ATR is the 14 day average of true range. Volatility tools measure how much price moves, never which way. A squeeze, very low bandwidth, for example about 4.7% before one stock's expansion, says a bigger move is due, not its direction. In strong trends, price walks the band, hugging an outer band; a close above the upper band still averaged plus 0.58% over 10 days, so it's strength, not a sell signal. ATR sets stops: 2 to 3 times ATR from entry. A stock with an ATR of about ₹85, 2.8% of its price, needs about ₹170 of room at 2 times ATR.", "The takeaway: Bollinger bands (20 SMA ± 2 SD) and ATR measure how much price moves, never which way.", "Pause and answer: What is a squeeze?", "⏸", "Answer: Very low bandwidth: volatility coiled before a move of unknown direction."]], ["volatility-phases-and-channel-tools", ["Volatility has three phases. In a squeeze, bandwidth and ATR are low and falling: price is coiling, and a move is likely, direction unknown. In expansion, they're rising and wide: the move is underway and may be maturing. In cooling, they're very wide but contracting: price is settling back towards balance. And four channel tools. Bollinger centres on the 20 SMA with standard deviation width, answering \"is price stretched?\". Keltner centres on an EMA with an ATR multiple, giving a smoother envelope. Donchian uses the N day high and low, typically 20, giving breakout levels. And ATR alone tells you the typical daily move in rupees. Use one volatility tool, not the whole set.", "The takeaway: Squeeze, expansion, cooling; Bollinger uses standard deviation, Keltner uses ATR, Donchian uses highs and lows.", "Pause and answer: Low and falling bandwidth implies what?", "⏸", "Answer: A squeeze: a move is likely, direction unknown."]], ["trend-strength-adx-dmi-and-aroon", ["Trend strength. ADX, from 0 to 100, measures strength only, not direction. Below 20 is weak or choppy, above 25 is a real trend, rising means it's gaining force, and around 40 or more is very strong and possibly mature. DMI adds direction: plus DI, upward pressure, above minus DI means buyers lead. A plus DI cross with ADX above 25 means much more than the same cross with ADX below 20. Aroon Up and Down, from 0 to 100, measure how recently a new high or low was made: Up near 100 with Down low is a fresh uptrend; both mid range is sideways. ADX lags, so use it as a context filter, never a timing trigger, and a very high ADX can mark a stretched trend near its end. In short: direction tells you which way; strength tells you whether to bother.", "The takeaway: ADX measures strength, not direction: under 20 is weak, over 25 a real trend; DMI shows who leads.", "Pause and answer: ADX at 30 and rising: what does it tell you?", "⏸", "Answer: A real trend gaining force, direction unknown from ADX alone."]], ["trend-or-range-pick-the-tactic", ["Trend or range? Pick the tactic. In a strong trend, ADX is above 25 and rising, the DMI lines are wide apart, and Aroon is pinned at an extreme. Let trend tools lead and trail your stops. In a choppy range, ADX is below 20, the DMI lines are tangled and crossing, and Aroon drifts mid range. Use range tactics at support and resistance, and switch the trend tools off.", "The takeaway: ADX above 25 means trend tools and trailing stops; below 20 means range tactics and trend tools off.", "Pause and answer: ADX below 20 with tangled DMI lines: which tactic?", "⏸", "Answer: Range tactics at support and resistance."]], ["trend-tools-that-sit-on-price", ["Three trend overlays sit on price. Supertrend is one line an ATR based distance from price that ratchets behind a rising price; it's great in clean trends as a trailing stop, but in ranges it whipsaws; one stock flipped 25 times in two years. Parabolic SAR is a series of dots that tighten towards price, and a touch flips it; good for steady trends and tight trailing, poor in ranges. And the Ichimoku cloud: above the cloud is bullish, below is bearish, inside is undecided. It gives slow regime context but fails when price hovers in the cloud. Check ADX first. Above about 25, read the overlay's side and trail the stop with it. Below about 20, ignore the flips. And stacking all three is one opinion drawn three ways.", "The takeaway: Supertrend, Parabolic SAR and Ichimoku work in clean trends and whipsaw in ranges.", "Pause and answer: Where does Supertrend fail?", "⏸", "Answer: In ranges: one stock flipped 25 times in two years."]], ["stacking-indicators-without-fooling-yourself", ["Stacking indicators without fooling yourself. There are four families, each answering a question with a job in your plan. Trend asks which way, and acts as a filter, for example no buys unless price is above a rising long average; pick a 50 day average or Supertrend. Momentum asks how strong and fast, and handles timing, like momentum turning up after a dip; pick RSI or MACD. Volume asks who's behind it and gives conviction; pick volume against its 20 day average, or OBV. Volatility asks how much and handles risk, setting stop distance and size; pick ATR or bandwidth. Name a tool's family before adding it; if you already own that family, skip it. Fill missing families first, usually volume and volatility. Four tools at most, and most charts need one or two. If two indicators always fire together, one is clutter. Conflict, like trend up but momentum overbought, is information: wait for a pullback. And price beats any oscillator: a break of a major level on heavy volume overrules \"overbought\".", "The takeaway: Pick one indicator per family (trend, momentum, volume, volatility), each with a job in the plan.", "Pause and answer: Why not use RSI and stochastic together?", "⏸", "Answer: Both are momentum: the same opinion repeated."]], ["a-clean-chart-template", ["A clean chart template. Candlesticks as the primary signal. Hand drawn support, resistance and trendlines. One trend tool, for example the 50 day average. One volume read, either volume bars with a 20 day average, or OBV. And optionally, one momentum tool or one volatility tool, not both. The test: you should be able to state the job of every line on your chart.", "The takeaway: Candles, hand-drawn levels, one trend tool, one volume read, and optionally one momentum or volatility tool.", "Pause and answer: What's the primary signal on a clean chart?", "⏸", "Answer: Candlesticks (price)."]], ["the-indicator-toolbox-seven-families", ["The indicator toolbox has seven families. Trend asks which way price is heading: moving averages, Supertrend, ADX, Ichimoku, Parabolic SAR. Momentum asks how strong and fast: RSI, MACD, rate of change. Volatility asks how much: Bollinger Bands, ATR, Keltner, Donchian. Volume asks who's behind the move: volume, OBV, MFI, Chaikin money flow, VWAP. Oscillators ask if price is stretched: stochastic, CCI, Williams percent R, stochastic RSI. Statistical tools ask what the numbers say: linear regression, correlation, beta, standard deviation. And hybrids do two jobs at once, like Supertrend, Ichimoku, ADX and SAR. OpenAlgo's library has more than 80 indicators, but most are variations on a few ideas built from the same past prices and volume. Learn the four everyday questions, which way, how strong, how much, and who, and treat the rest as a reference shelf, not a to do list.", "The takeaway: Trend, momentum, volatility, volume and more: each family answers a different question.", "Pause and answer: Which family answers 'who is behind the move?'", "⏸", "Answer: Volume."]]], "action": [[null, ["Section six: from analysis to action. Timeframes, systems, risk and discipline. This is where analysis becomes a process."]], ["top-down-two-timeframes-two-jobs", ["Top down analysis uses two timeframes with two jobs. Never decide from one timeframe. Use two about 4 to 5 times apart, like weekly and daily for swing trading, since one weekly candle is about five daily ones. The weekly sets direction: is the structure making higher highs and lows, where is price against a long average and its slope, and where are the major support and resistance levels? The daily sets timing: the location, ideally a pullback to support or a rising average rather than a stretched price, and a trigger in the direction the weekly allows. If they conflict, the larger timeframe wins, which usually means no trade. A 4% two day drop looks severe on the daily and minor on the weekly.", "The takeaway: Use two timeframes 4–5× apart: weekly for direction, daily for location and trigger.", "Pause and answer: What does the weekly chart decide?", "⏸", "Answer: Direction: structure, price versus a long average, major levels."]], ["the-five-step-timeframe-checklist", ["This five step checklist starts with a swing trade idea on a daily chart. One: is the weekly trend up, with higher highs and higher lows? If not, no long trade; when the weekly is sideways or down, don't buy dips. Two: is there room to the next major weekly resistance? If not, wait, because you'd be buying into a weekly wall. Three: is the daily location a pullback to support or a rising average? If not, wait for a pullback, since stretched entries have the weakest odds. Four: is there a daily trigger in the weekly direction? If not, keep it on the watchlist: you have location without a trigger. If all four pass, the timeframes are aligned: act, with a stop below the pullback low, and size from the stop. Most setups should fail this checklist. That's the point.", "The takeaway: Weekly uptrend, room to weekly resistance, daily pullback location, a trigger, and a stop with reward at least 2×.", "Pause and answer: Weekly trend is sideways. Buy daily dips?", "⏸", "Answer: No long trade."]], ["from-setup-to-system", ["From setup to system. Four things must be fixed before trading. Context: when is a trade allowed, given trend, timeframe and market mood? Trigger: what exact event enters? Exit: a target or trailing rule when right, and a stop when wrong. Size: how much to commit, from the stop. Write the rule so a stranger would take the same trades. Backtest across many stocks and years, including costs. Read the equity curve and worst drawdown, not just the headline return. Resist tuning: prefer few rules and round numbers, like 50 and 200 rather than 47 and 213, and check on data the rule never saw. And decide what you're actually buying: return, smoothness, or time out of the market.", "The takeaway: Fix context, trigger, exit and size before trading, written so a stranger would take the same trades.", "Pause and answer: Name the four parts of a system.", "⏸", "Answer: Context, trigger, exit, size."]], ["expectancy-beats-win-rate", ["Expectancy beats win rate. Expectancy is the win rate times the average win, minus the loss rate times the average loss. With a 40% win rate, ₹2 average wins and ₹1 average losses, that's 0.4 times 2 minus 0.6 times 1, plus ₹0.20 per ₹1 risked. So a 40% win rate is profitable when winners are about twice the losers: cut losers at 1R and let winners run to 2 or 3R. The course's 50 and 200 trend filter on 48 stocks returned a median of 107% against 744% for buy and hold, with drawdown of 47% versus 56%, invested about 55% of the time. Protection from big falls also costs you the sharpest recoveries. And be suspicious of a silky smooth, loss free backtest; it's usually curve fitted.", "The takeaway: A 40% win rate is profitable when winners are about twice losers: cut at 1R, let winners run.", "Pause and answer: 40% wins at 2R, 60% losses at 1R. Expectancy per ₹1 risked?", "⏸", "Answer: +₹0.20."]], ["position-size-from-the-stop", ["Position size comes from the stop. Put the stop where the trade's reason is invalidated, a little beyond the level, never exactly on it. Set the risk budget at 1 to 2% of capital. Check the target is at least about twice the stop distance; below 1 to 1, pass. Then size equals the budget divided by entry minus stop. Execute, and honour the stop; never widen it. The worked example: entry 322.8, stop 300, so the risk per share, R, is 22.8. With a target of 380, reward to risk is about 2.5. On ₹10 lakh capital, 1% is ₹10,000, and 10,000 divided by 22.8 is about 438 shares. That position is about ₹1.41 lakh, 14% of capital, and if stopped out you lose exactly the planned ₹10,000.", "The takeaway: Stop beyond the invalidation level, risk 1–2%, target at least 2R, size = budget ÷ (entry − stop).", "Pause and answer: Entry 322.8, stop 300, target 380: reward to risk?", "⏸", "Answer: About 2.5."]], ["survival-maths", ["Survival maths. A 10% drawdown needs an 11% gain to recover. 20% needs 25%. 33% needs 50%. 50% needs 100%. 75% needs 300%, and 90% needs 900%. At 1% risk per trade, ten straight losses cost about 10%. At 20% risk per trade, three losses cost about half the account. Risk is the input; position size is the output.", "The takeaway: Deep drawdowns need huge gains to recover; at 1% risk ten straight losses cost about 10%.", "Pause and answer: Gain needed to recover from −33%?", "⏸", "Answer: +50%."]], ["emotions-and-the-rules-they-break", ["Emotions, and the rules they break. Fear breaks \"let winners run and honour stops\", leaving you with small wins and big losses. Greed breaks the fixed 1% risk rule; a 5% bet means one loss costs five planned losses. FOMO breaks \"wait for your entry\", leading to buying after a 6% news gap, where the odds are weakest. Overtrading breaks \"trade only your setups\", and costs erase a thin edge. Curve fitting breaks \"keep rules simple\" and gives false confidence. Most retail losses come from behaviour, not analysis: thin edges minus costs, emotional errors and overtrading usually land below zero. Discipline is learnable; prediction is not.", "The takeaway: Fear, greed, FOMO, overtrading and curve-fitting each break a specific rule.", "Pause and answer: Which emotion breaks 'let winners run'?", "⏸", "Answer: Fear."]], ["plan-execute-journal", ["The loop is plan, execute, journal, review. Plan while calm, in writing: instruments, timeframe, setups, exact entry, stop, target and minimum reward to risk, risk of 1 to 2%, and conduct rules like a daily loss cap, no chasing gaps, and no widening stops. Execute only planned setups. Journal every trade: the setup, the reason, the stop and target, whether you followed the plan, and your emotion. Then review the last 20 trades on process, and feed that back into the plan. Grade process, not profit. A winner taken on a whim is still a bad trade.", "The takeaway: Plan while calm, execute only planned setups, journal every trade, review the last 20 on process.", "Pause and answer: How many trades does the review look at?", "⏸", "Answer: The last 20, judged on process."]], ["the-automation-path", ["The automation path runs in four steps: write the rules, backtest over years, paper trade in a sandbox, then go live at small size. OpenAlgo's analyzer mode is a sandbox that trades with no money at risk. Automation buys consistency, the same rules on good days and bad, not guaranteed profit.", "The takeaway: Write rules, backtest, paper trade in the analyzer sandbox, then go live small; automation buys consistency, not profit.", "Pause and answer: What comes between backtesting and going live?", "⏸", "Answer: Paper trading in a sandbox (OpenAlgo's analyzer mode)."]]], "last": [[null, ["Final section: checklists and formulas. Run these before every trade and at every weekly review."]], ["before-the-trade", ["Before the trade. The weekly direction allows it, and the daily location and trigger agree. The setup sits at a level marked in advance, with the trend, on above average volume. ADX tells you whether to use trend tools or range tactics, and you're using the right ones. The stop is beyond the invalidation level and the target at least about twice the stop. Size is 1 to 2% of capital divided by entry minus stop. And there's no major news or results before the planned exit.", "The takeaway: Weekly direction, daily location and trigger, a level with trend and volume, the right tactic for ADX, a 2R target, and risk-based size.", "Pause and answer: What target is required relative to the stop?", "⏸", "Answer: At least about 2× the stop distance."]], ["honest-signal-checklist", ["The honest signal checklist. Compared with the 1.48% per 20 days baseline, does this signal add anything? Is it one tool per family, or the same opinion repeated? Has the rule been tested on many stocks and years, with costs? Would you take the same trade if you'd written the rule down first? And are you chasing a breakout, or waiting for the retest?", "The takeaway: Does the signal beat baseline drift, is it one tool per family, was it tested with costs, and are you chasing?", "Pause and answer: What baseline must a signal beat?", "⏸", "Answer: About +1.48% per 20 days."]], ["one-line-formulas", ["The one line formulas. Shares equal risk budget divided by entry minus stop. Reward to risk is target minus entry, divided by entry minus stop. Expectancy is win rate times average win minus loss rate times average loss. Recovery needed is 1 divided by 1 minus the drawdown, minus 1. An ATR stop sits at entry minus 2 to 3 times ATR. Relative volume is volume divided by the 20 day average. And gap percentage is the open minus the previous close, divided by the previous close, times 100.", "The takeaway: Shares = risk ÷ (entry − stop); R:R = reward ÷ risk; recovery = 1 ÷ (1 − drawdown) − 1.", "Pause and answer: Gap % formula?", "⏸", "Answer: (Open − previous close) ÷ previous close × 100."]], ["about-this-sheet", ["This sheet condenses the free Technical Analysis course from OpenAlgo Open Varsity, 28 chapters in 6 modules, into reference cards in our own words. The backtest figures are the course's own, on 16 years of daily Nifty 50 data, and single stock examples are illustrations, not evidence. Each section links back to its chapters. It's educational, not investment advice. That completes the sheet. Thanks for listening."]]]};
